---
// LEGAL LINE (spec §6). This page positions Coastal as the homeowner's resource:
// documenting the loss and working the repair scope with the adjuster. That is all
// standard contractor work.
//
// What must NOT appear here, no matter how the copy is reworded later: any claim
// that Coastal negotiates, adjusts, settles, or handles the claim itself. Under
// Fla. Stat. 489.147 a contractor may not negotiate a claim on an insured's behalf,
// and advertising it is unlicensed public adjusting (Fla. Stat. 626.854) — a
// licensing and criminal exposure, not a style preference.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/TrustBar.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Insurance Claim Help | Storm & Water Damage Claims | Coastal Property Restorations"
  description="Insurance companies have adjusters, engineers, and attorneys. Most homeowners have none of that. Coastal documents your damage and works the repair scope with the adjuster across Southwest Florida."
>
  <TrustBar />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 46rem;">
      <h1>You Shouldn't Have to Do This Alone</h1>
      <p>
        When you file a property damage claim, the insurance company brings a team.
        Staff adjusters who handle hundreds of losses a year. Engineers. Attorneys.
        People who do this every single day and know exactly what a claim file
        should look like.
      </p>
      <p>
        You're doing it for the first time, from a house that just got wrecked,
        while trying to keep your family somewhere dry. That's not a fair fight —
        and homeowners who don't have the resources or the know-how are the ones who
        end up shortchanged.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>That's where we come in.</strong> We've walked Southwest Florida
        homeowners through this more times than we can count. You get someone on
        your side of the table who speaks the language, knows what the damage
        actually takes to repair, and won't let anything get overlooked.
      </p>

      <h2>What we bring to your side</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Complete documentation.</strong> Photos of every affected area, moisture readings logged daily, and a detailed written scope — the file your loss deserves.</li>
        <li><strong>We know what repairs really require.</strong> Florida building code, hidden damage, what has to come out versus what can be saved. We put it in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>We deal with the adjuster on the scope.</strong> When it's time to go through what your home needs, we're the ones walking it with them — not you.</li>
        <li><strong>One company start to finish.</strong> The same people who documented the damage do the repairs, so nothing gets lost in a handoff.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the process looks like</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Call us first.</strong> Before anything else. We'll tell you what to do right now, and get a crew moving.</li>
        <li><strong>We stop the damage and document it.</strong> Tarping, water extraction, dry-out — photographed and recorded from the first hour.</li>
        <li><strong>We build the file.</strong> Full photo record, moisture logs, and a line-by-line scope of what it takes to put your home back.</li>
        <li><strong>The inspection.</strong> When the adjuster walks the property, we're there with the documentation and we go through the scope with them.</li>
        <li><strong>We rebuild.</strong> Repairs to the agreed scope, then a final walkthrough with you and your complete documentation package.</li>
      </ol>

      <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <strong>The most important thing you can do is call early.</strong> The
        documentation that protects you starts the moment we walk in — and the
        sooner that is, the more complete your record.
        <a href={site.phoneHref}>Call {site.phone}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQ
    items={[
      { q: 'Should I call a contractor or the insurance company first?', a: 'Call us first. We can start protecting the property immediately and get the damage documented properly from the first hour — that record is what everything else is built on. We will walk you through what comes next so nothing catches you off guard.' },
      { q: 'Do I have to handle the adjuster myself?', a: 'No. When the adjuster walks your property, we are there with the full documentation and we go through the repair scope with them. You should not have to argue about roof decking or moisture readings with someone who does this for a living.' },
      { q: 'What documentation do you provide?', a: 'Photos of every affected area from the first hour forward, daily moisture readings during dry-out, and a detailed line-by-line scope of the repairs. It goes to you as a complete package at the end of the job.' },
      { q: 'Will my rates go up if I file?', a: 'Nobody can honestly promise you either way — that comes down to your insurer and your history. What we can tell you plainly is what the damage actually takes to repair and what that should cost. Call us and we will give you the straight version before you decide anything.' },
      { q: 'What is a deductible?', a: 'The portion of the loss you pay before your coverage picks up the rest. In Florida, hurricane deductibles are often a percentage of your home\'s insured value rather than a flat dollar amount, which catches a lot of people off guard. If you are not sure how yours works, call us and we will help you make sense of it.' },
    ]}
  />

  <CTABand heading="Talk to us before you talk to anyone else." />
</Base>
